<header><p class="enc-kicker">Person</p><h1 class="enc-title">Mongkut</h1><p class="enc-meta"><span class="dates"><a href="/years/1804">1804</a>–<a href="/years/1868">1868</a></span> · <a href="/centuries/19th-century">19th century</a> · Also known as Rama IV</p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">Mongkut, King <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/rama">Rama</a> IV of Siam, reigned from 1851 to 1868. Before his accession he spent 27 years as a Buddhist monk, founding the reformist <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/dhammayuttika-nikaya">Thammayut order</a> and studying <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/latin">Latin</a>, English and Western science. As king he signed the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/bowring-treaty">Bowring Treaty</a> of 1855 and similar treaties that opened Siam to Western trade, and he died of malaria caught on an expedition to observe a solar eclipse.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/mongkut.jpg" alt="Mongkut" width="320" height="466" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: John Thomson · CC0 · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:First_King_of_Siam_MET_DP-573-001_(cropped).jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
<section class="enc-more"><h2 class="enc-more-h">Early life and background</h2><p>Mongkut, whose name means crown, was born on 18 October 1804, the second son of Prince Itsarasunthon, who was crowned <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/rama-ii">Rama II</a> in 1809, and Princess Bunrot. In 1824 he became a Buddhist monk, as Siamese custom asked of young men, and in the same year his father died. The nobility passed him over for the older and more experienced Prince Chetsadabodin, known as <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/rama-iii">Nangklao</a>, and Mongkut stayed in the monastery. In 1835 he began a reform movement that became the Dhammayut sect.</p><p>In 1836 he became first abbot of Wat Bowonniwet, where he studied <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/latin">Latin</a>, English and astronomy with missionaries and sailors and became friends with Vicar Pallegoix. In 1829 at Phetchaburi he had met the monk Buddhawangso, who followed the vinaya, the monastic discipline, strictly.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Reign</h2><p>Dit Bunnag, the most powerful noble of the previous reign, and British merchants backed Mongkut, who left the monkhood and took the throne in 1851 at the age of 47. He took the name Phra Chom Klao and crowned his brother Isaret as second king, Pinklao. The <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/bunnag-family">Bunnag family</a> was at its height under him. He ordered nobles to wear shirts at court, ended the practice of sending envoys to the Qing court, banned forced marriages and the sale of wives to pay debts, and released many royal concubines. He had 32 wives and 82 children. He hired missionaries to teach English to the princes and urged a European-style education. Siamese forces failed to take <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/kengtung">Kengtung</a> in 1855 after a siege of 21 days.</p><p>John Bowring came to Siam in 1854 to negotiate a treaty. It abolished the Royal Storage, which had held the monopoly on foreign trade since <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ayutthaya-kingdom">Ayutthaya</a>, and limited import tax to 3 per cent, collected once. Trade, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/rice">rice</a> growing, milling and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/sugar-plantation">sugar</a> production expanded, and coinage was introduced in 1860. The treaty also gave British subjects extraterritoriality, and more treaties with other powers followed, undermining national revenue and legal rights. In 1862 the court hired Anna Leonowens, whose memoir became the source of The King and I.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Death and legacy</h2><p>Mongkut studied indigenous astrology and Western astronomy and predicted the total solar eclipse of 18 August 1868. He invited European and Siamese officials to watch it at Wakor, south of Hua Hin, and his calculations proved accurate. He and his son Prince <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/chulalongkorn">Chulalongkorn</a> caught malaria on the expedition, and the king died six weeks later, on 1 October 1868. Chulalongkorn, who survived, succeeded him, and in 1902 gave the Thammayut sect royal recognition, making it one of the two major Buddhist denominations of modern <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/thailand">Thailand</a>.</p><p class="enc-more-src">This account follows the Wikipedia article “Mongkut” (CC BY-SA 4.0), linked under Read further below.</p></section>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote><b>Mongkut</b> spent 27 years as a Buddhist monk before becoming king in 1851, founding the reformist Thammayut order and studying Western astronomy.</blockquote><a href="/empire/rattanakosin-kingdom">Rattanakosin Kingdom · At a glance</a></li><li><blockquote>Modern Thai historiography, shaped from the nineteenth century by King <b>Mongkut</b>, who found the Ram Khamhaeng inscription in 1833, and by twentieth-century nationalist historians, made Sukhothai the first Thai kingdom and the model of benevolent, Buddhist rule.</blockquote><a href="/empire/sukhothai-kingdom">Sukhothai Kingdom · Global &amp; Regional Impact</a></li><li><blockquote>In the 1840s King <b>Mongkut</b> of Thailand invented the Ariyaka script as a universal medium for Pali, but it never came into popular use.</blockquote><a href="/language/pali">Pali · Sounds, Script &amp; Grammar</a></li><li><blockquote>Its history begins in the reign of King <b>Mongkut</b>.</blockquote><a href="/songs/sansoen-phra-barami">Sansoen Phra Barami</a></li></ul></section>
<section class="enc-where enc-born"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Born and died</h2><p class="enc-born-p">Born in Thonburi Province, in what is now <a href="/countries/thailand">Thailand</a>. Died in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/grand-palace">Grand Palace</a>, in what is now <a href="/countries/thailand">Thailand</a>. <span class="enc-born-src">Places as Wikidata records them.</span></p><figure class="st-locator enc-loc"><a href="/atlas?at=13.73,100.49&amp;year=1845" aria-label="Thonburi Province on the atlas"><svg class="map map-globe map-loc" viewBox="0 0 10000 10000" role="img" aria-label="Globe: the birthplace of Mongkut"><image href="/maps/globes/g09000.svg" x="0" y="0" width="10000" height="10000"/><path class="map-dot-halo" d="M5866 3837h0"/><path class="map-dot-me" d="M5866 3837h0"/></svg></a><figcaption>The dot marks the birthplace. <a href="/atlas?at=13.73,100.49&amp;year=1845">What was here in 1845? &rarr;</a></figcaption></figure></section>
